  "content": {
    "id": "p10-q29-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "29",
    "prompt": "Prove that for any $n\\in\\mathbb{N}$, there exists a sequence of $n$ consecutive composite numbers.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "One such sequence is $(n+1)!+2,\\ (n+1)!+3,\\ldots,\\ (n+1)!+(n+1)$.\n\nEach term $(n+1)!+k$ has the proper divisor $k$, for $2\\le k\\le n+1$.",
      "worked": "Consider the sequence\n$$ (n+1)!+2,\\quad (n+1)!+3,\\quad\\ldots,\\quad (n+1)!+(n+1). $$\nIt contains $n$ consecutive integers. For every integer $k$ with $2\\le k\\le n+1$, the factorial $(n+1)!$ is divisible by $k$. Hence\n$$ (n+1)!+k=k\\left(\\frac{(n+1)!}{k}+1\\right). $$\nBoth factors are integers greater than $1$, so every term is composite. Thus there exists a sequence of $n$ consecutive composite numbers.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 60
  },
